tural  gas and particularly of natural gas  coming from russia and they have done  you know significant uh progress today  but to say it's going to turn its back  entirely on it it's not just germany  many european countries and many  countries around the world as well it's  going to take a while this is the  reality of the gas business  unfortunately so even with the political  motivation uh to turn away from russian  gas germany will still need uh kremlin  approved gas in the future for that  matter  well in the future again this is the  question of time so in the future the  longer term there's plenty of gas around  and not necessarily coming from russia  it just takes time to take that gas out  of the ground put it you know even  pipeline or on ships like lng they could  find natural gas you do have available  but it just takes time to build the  terminals to export it and even if they  are there you need the terminals to  import it and that's what you don't have  in german this is going to be built but  when you have more energy coming to  germany then you don't really need  russian gas you can do it easily without  russian gas but that will take time  now there are people who are fearing a  gas shortage when it comes uh to heating  season in in the coming months  not only in germany but in other  countries in europe as well should it  come to that who should be prioritized  companies or households  well look it's a question of balancing  economics with morality and political  interest but usually i think most  governments would agree that households  should be given the priority and then  industries will have to cope even the  economic cost might be much more  significant but if i were to answer it  from a moral perspective i would say  households should come first  big efforts are underway in europe as i  mentioned to move away from russian gas  but if we look at other parts in the  world what does the picture there look  like  well it seems that you see it's